::ALG2_U04_L04_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_2(8)?
{
=A. 3#Correct. 2 raised to the third power equals 8.
~B. 2#2 squared is 4, not 8.
~C. 4#2 to the fourth power is 16.
~D. 16#This is not the exponent needed.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_3(81)?
{
~A. 3#3 cubed is 27.
=B. 4#Correct. 3 to the fourth power equals 81.
~C. 9#3 to the ninth power is much larger than 81.
~D. 27#This is not the exponent needed.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_10(1000)?
{
~A. 2#10 squared equals 100.
~B. 10#10 to the tenth power is too large.
=C. 3#Correct. 10 cubed equals 1000.
~D. 100#This is not the exponent needed.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which exponential form matches log_4(64)=3?
{
~A. 64³ = 4#This reverses input and base.
~B. 3⁴ = 64#This uses the output as the base.
~C. 4 + 3 = 64#Logarithms convert to exponent equations, not addition.
=D. 4³ = 64#Correct. The base 4 raised to the logarithm output 3 equals 64.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which logarithmic form matches 5² = 25?
{
=A. log_5(25)=2#Correct. The base is 5, the input is 25, and the output is 2.
~B. log_25(5)=2#This reverses base and input.
~C. log_5(2)=25#This makes the exponent the input.
~D. log_2(25)=5#This uses the exponent as the base.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is the domain of y=log_2(x)?
{
~A. x >= 0#Zero is not allowed.
=B. x > 0#Correct. Logarithmic inputs must be positive.
~C. all real numbers#Negative and zero inputs are not in the basic log domain.
~D. x != 2#The base does not create this restriction.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is the range of y=log_2(x)?
{
~A. x > 0#That describes the domain, not the range.
~B. y > 0#Logarithm outputs can be negative, zero, or positive.
=C. all real numbers#Correct. Logarithmic outputs can be any real number.
~D. y != 0#The output can be 0 when x = 1.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is the vertical asymptote of y=log_3(x)?
{
~A. y = 0#That is the horizontal asymptote of the exponential inverse.
~B. x = 3#The base does not become the vertical asymptote.
~C. y = 3#This does not describe the asymptote.
=D. x = 0#Correct. The basic logarithmic graph approaches the y-axis.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which point is on y=log_2(x)?
{
=A. (8, 3)#Correct. Because log_2(8)=3.
~B. (3, 8)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (2, 8)#log_2(2)=1, not 8.
~D. (0, 2)#x=0 is not in the domain.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_4(1)?
{
~A. 1#4 to the first power is 4.
=B. 0#Correct. Any positive base to the zero power equals 1.
~C. 4#4 to the fourth power is 256.
~D. undefined#The logarithm of 1 is defined for valid positive bases not equal to 1.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_7(7)?
{
~A. 0#7 to the zero power equals 1.
~B. 7#7 to the seventh power is too large.
=C. 1#Correct. 7 to the first power equals 7.
~D. 49#This is not the needed exponent.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which statement is true about y=log_2(x) and y=2ˣ?
{
~A. They are the same graph.#Inverse graphs reflect across y = x, so they are not identical.
~B. Both have domain x > 0.#The exponential domain is all real numbers.
~C. Both have range y > 0.#The logarithmic range is all real numbers.
=D. They are inverse functions.#Correct. They reverse inputs and outputs.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_5(125)?
{
=A. 3#Correct. 5 cubed equals 125.
~B. 2#5 squared equals 25.
~C. 4#5 to the fourth is 625.
~D. 25#This is not the exponent needed.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which form matches log_b(a)=c?
{
~A. aᶜ = b#This reverses the base and input.
=B. bᶜ = a#Correct. The base raised to the log output equals the input.
~C. b + c = a#The relationship is exponential, not additive.
~D. cᵇ = a#This uses the output as the base.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_2(1/8)?
{
~A. 3#2 cubed equals 8, not 1/8.
~B. -8#This is not the exponent needed.
=C. -3#Correct. 2 to the negative third power equals 1/8.
~D. 1/3#2 to the one-third power is not 1/8.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_9(9)?
{
~A. 0#9 to the zero power equals 1.
~B. 2#9 squared equals 81.
~C. 18#This is not the exponent needed.
=D. 1#Correct. 9 to the first power equals 9.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which graph feature belongs to y=log_10(x)?
{
=A. Vertical asymptote x = 0#Correct. Basic logarithmic functions approach x = 0 from the right.
~B. Horizontal asymptote y = 0#That belongs to the exponential inverse.
~C. Vertex at (0,0)#Logarithmic graphs do not have a vertex.
~D. Domain all real numbers#The domain is positive x-values.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What is log_6(36)?
{
~A. 3#6 cubed equals 216.
=B. 2#Correct. 6 squared equals 36.
~C. 6#6 to the sixth power is much larger.
~D. 30#This subtracts instead of using exponents.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
Which logarithm equals 4?
{
~A. log_4(2)#4 to the one-half power equals 2, not 4.
~B. log_16(2)#16 to the one-fourth power equals 2.
=C. log_2(16)#Correct. 2 to the fourth power equals 16.
~D. log_2(8)#This equals 3.
}


::ALG2_U04_L04_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L04_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.2
Question:
What does log_3(x)=5 mean?
{
~A. x⁵ = 3#This reverses the input and base.
~B. 3x = 5#This treats the relationship as multiplication.
~C. x + 3 = 5#This treats the relationship as addition.
=D. 3⁵ = x#Correct. The log output is the exponent on base 3.
}

